 "literal": "To the north of the unfading, snow-capped tall mountain / To the south of the fear-inspiring Kumari / To the east of the shore-battering dug-out sea / To the west of the ancient, old ocean / Below, even below the depth of the water-world of the three-fold joined ordered first structure / Above, even in the world of cows, unceasingly / Having become form and fame, like the examining pointer of a balance scale, without bias / May your side excel! / In the land of the enemies who opposed your actions of war / Your ocean-like army submerging, surging, commanding properly the dense, spotted, small-eyed elephants / Capturing many difficult fortresses with green-pastured gardens / Bestowing the fine ornaments obtained from those fortresses to the needy people according to their merit / May your umbrella bow down only to circumambulate the temple of the three-eyed Lord! / May your head bow down, O great one, before the raised hands of the sages of the four Vedas! / May your garland wither, O Lord, only because of the blowing of the fragrant smoke from burning the enemies' lands! / May your anger go away only before the bright faces of your sulking, bright-jewelled women! / Thus, having conquered all victories and contained them within / O Kuṭumi of glorious quality and unceasing generosity! / Like the cool-rayed moon and like the scorching-rayed, bright sun / May you endure upon this earth, O great one!",
 "translation": "To the north of the unfading, snow-capped Himalayas, to the south of the awe-inspiring Kumari, to the east of the shore-battering ocean, and to the west of the ancient sea; below the depths of the underworld, and above the heavens, your form and fame spread unceasingly. Like the needle of a balanced scale, you rule without bias; may your reign excel! You sent your ocean-like army to submerge the lands of the enemies who opposed you, commanding your spotted, small-eyed elephants to capture their fortresses. The fine ornaments taken from those forts, you bestowed upon the needy according to their merit. May your royal umbrella bow only when circumambulating the temple of the three-eyed Lord Shiva! May your head bow, O great king, only before the raised hands of the sages of the four Vedas! May your garland wither only from the fragrant smoke of burning enemy lands! May your anger vanish only before the bright faces of your sulking, beautifully jewelled wives! O Kuṭumi of glorious quality and unceasing generosity, who has gathered all victories within yourself! Like the cool-rayed moon and the fierce, bright sun, may you endure forever upon this earth!",
